\p{\strong{11:41 AM}}
\hr
\p{\em{Today's [GEB](hofstadterGodelEscherBach1999).} The primary focus was on \em{figure} vs. \em{ground}. The concept was nicely introduced across a variety of disciplines. The mathematical point of view was in defining the formaly system of prime numbers. It is easy to do so as the \em{ground} of composite numbers. Here, the \em{ground} is the set of theorems not included in the formal system of composite numbers. It posed the very interesting question of whether all \em{grounds} can be rewritten as \em{figures}. Surprisingly, this is not the case in mathematics (though it is the case for prime numbers). This notion of \em{figure} is then translated to its logical counterpart \em{recursively enumerable}. A \em{recursive set} is then a set whose \em{ground} and \em{figure} are both \em{recursively enumerable}.}

